WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:10.720
Today, we're going to discuss a great piece
of work that shows us how efficient and versatile

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.610
neural network-based techniques had become
recently.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:19.820
Here, the input is a bunch or RGB-D images,
which are photographs endowed with depth information,

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:25.270
and the output can be a full 3D reconstruction
of a scene, and much, much more, which we'll

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.490
see in a moment.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.580
This task is typically taken care of by handcrafting
descriptors.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.879
A descriptor is a specialized representation
for doing useful tasks on images and other

00:00:35.879 --> 00:00:37.200
data structures.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.309
For instance, if we seek to build an algorithm
to recognize black and white images, a useful

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:47.199
descriptor would definitely contain the number
of colors that are visible in an image, and

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:48.679
a list of these colors.

00:00:48.679 --> 00:00:53.539
Again, these descriptors have been typically
handcrafted by scientists for decades.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:56.399
New problem, new descriptors, new papers.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:02.690
But not this time, because here, super effective
descriptors are proposed automatically via

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.600
a learning algorithm, a convolutional neural
network and siamese networks.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.990
This is incredible!

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:14.549
Creating such descriptors took extremely smart
researchers and years of work on a specific

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:18.149
problem, and were still often not as good
as these ones.

00:01:18.149 --> 00:01:23.020
By the way, we have discussed siamese networks
in an earlier episode, as always, the link

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.329
is available in the video description.

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:29.460
And as you can imagine, several really cool
applications emerge from this.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.409
One, when combined with RANSAC, a technique
used to find order in noisy measurement data,

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:39.829
it is able to perform 3D scene reconstruction
from just a few images.

00:01:39.829 --> 00:01:44.470
And it completely smokes the competition.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:50.140
Two, pose estimation with bounding boxes.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.490
Given a sample of an object, the algorithm
is able to recognize not only the shape itself,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.479
but also its orientation when given a scene
cluttered with other objects.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:03.859
Three, correspondance search is possible.

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:05.799
This is really cool!

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:11.480
This means that a semantically similar piece
of geometry is recognized on different objects.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.390
For instance, the algorithm can learn the
concept of a handle, and recognize the handles

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:22.599
on a variety of objects, such as on motorcycles,
carriages, chairs, and more!

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:25.799
The source code of this project is also available.

00:02:25.799 --> 00:02:26.870
Yoohoo!

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.760
Neural networks are rapidly establishing supremacy
in a number of research fields, and I am so

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.819
happy to be alive in this age of incredible
research progress.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:41.060
Make sure to subscribe to the series and click
the bell icon, some amazing works are coming

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.909
up in the next few episodes, and there will
be lots of fun to be had.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:49.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

